---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Team from '../components/Team.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { defaultLocale } from '../lib/i18n';

/*
  The team has its own page because of its height, not its weight.

  As five alternating photo-and-paragraph rows the section measured 3249px at
  1366x768 — 4.2 viewports — and 4507px on a 390x844 phone, 5.3 viewports. That
  was roughly a third of the homepage in one band, and it sat between Our Goal
  and From the kitchen, so anyone scrolling to the food went through all five
  bios to get there. Moving it out is the alternative to shortening it, which
  the client had already declined once: the portrait grid that used to keep it
  to 1030px put the bios in ~220px columns, about four words a line, and was
  reverted for that reason. On its own page the rows can stay full width and
  cost the homepage nothing.

  Two knock-on effects, both checked rather than assumed:

  - The homepage band order still alternates. It was black, stone, salt, black,
    salt, stone, black; without Team it is black, stone, black, salt, stone,
    black, so no two bands share a background and nothing had to be re-tiered.
  - The heading moved up a level. Team.astro renders an <h1> now, and the member
    names went h3 -> h2, because this page's only heading is the section's own
    and h1 -> h3 would skip a level. Both are class-styled, so nothing moved
    visually — see the note in CLAUDE.md about grepping the tag, not the class.

  No extra structured data. The Restaurant graph stays on the homepage; nothing
  in schema.org's Restaurant vocabulary describes a staff roster in a way that
  is worth 5 more entries, and the bios are prose rather than credentials.
*/
const locale = defaultLocale;
---

<Base locale={locale} titleKey="meta.teamTitle" descriptionKey="meta.teamDescription">
  <Nav locale={locale} />

  {/*
    id="main" is what the skip link targets. The footer's "back to top" uses
    href="#top", which needs no element — HTML treats the fragment "top" as the
    top of the document when nothing carries that id.
  */}
  <main id="main">
    <Team locale={locale} />
  </main>

  <Footer locale={locale} />
</Base>
